Follow-up to mlir_aie_vision_examples_npu.log, which explicitly deferred ml/magika and
ml/mobilenet as "multi-file models substantial enough to warrant their own log." This
log runs both, plus ml/resnet/layers_conv2_x -- a design not previously surveyed at all
(missed in the original vision/ml pass) that turns out to be tagged for this exact chip.

Same mlir-aie-iron conda env / C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie clone (tag v1.4.2) as prior logs.
`make clean` before every run below (fresh compiles, not cache hits).

--- ml/resnet/layers_conv2_x (new: not in the original vision/ml survey) ---

REQUIRES: ryzen_ai_npu1, peano, torch -- applies directly to this Phoenix machine, no
gaps. Three ResNet conv2_x-stage bottleneck blocks (conv1x1->conv3x3->conv1x1 + skip,
the same structural unit resnet/bottleneck already proved) chained depth-first across
three separate NPU columns, output handed column-to-column without going off-chip
between blocks -- the multi-column version of the single-block bottleneck design.

  make -f Makefile run_py  ->
    NPU time     (avg/min/max us): 1888.5 / 1850.1 / 1954.5
    End-to-end   (avg/min/max us): 2583.2 / 2328.4 / 2838.2
    PASS!

--- ml/magika (text-type classifier, https://github.com/google/magika) ---

REQUIRES (run_phoenix.lit): ryzen_ai_npu1, peano, torch -- applies to this machine.
The lit file carries "XFAIL: *" with the comment "Known-failing numerical check on the
NPU. Silently skipped on main (the torch feature was off), so mark as an expected
failure now that it runs." Ran every target in that lit by hand rather than trusting
the XFAIL label, since an XFAIL from upstream CI is not the same claim as "fails on
this machine" -- and it did not reproduce as a numerical failure here:

  group0 (kernels/group0.cc, GROUPA+GROUPB variants):
    make targetname=group0 run_py    -> Avg NPU time: 665us.  evm: -34.85  PASS.
    make targetname=group0 trace_py  -> Avg NPU time: 639us.  evm: -34.85  PASS
      (NPU dispatch identical to run_py; see trace note below)

  group2 (kernels/group2.cc):
    make targetname=group2 run_py    -> Avg NPU time: 455us.  evm: -56.91  PASS.
    make targetname=group2 trace_py  -> Avg NPU time: 463us.  evm: -56.91  PASS
      (NPU dispatch identical to run_py; see trace note below)

Both groups pass with large negative EVM (error-vector-magnitude in dB; more negative
is better -- -34.85 and -56.91 dB both indicate the AIE output tracks the reference
closely, not a marginal pass). The lit's XFAIL did not manifest as a numeric mismatch
in either group on this hardware/toolchain combination -- recorded as measured, not
assumed; the upstream expected-failure may be tied to a different environment, a
group1 combination not implemented here ("group1 component is not yet implemented"
per magika's own README), or may have since been fixed on the mlir-aie side.

Tooling gap, not a hardware result: `trace_py`'s NPU run and numeric check both pass
identically to run_py, but the downstream trace-JSON step then fails --
`python/utils/trace/parse.py` looks for
`build/aie_trace.mlir.prj/input_with_addresses.mlir`, and aiecc's own step log reports
"(4/37) input_with_addresses.mlir ... took 18 ms" but the file is not left on disk in
that .prj directory afterward (confirmed by listing the directory: 17 other
intermediates present, this one absent). This is the trace-visualization post-step
only; the actual NPU compute and numerical PASS above are unaffected.

Compile-flag note: magika's Makefile (unlike bottleneck/conv2d, which route through
makefile-common's `jit_xclbin` macro) defines its own `build/%.o` clang rule that
expands `${MLIR_AIE_DIR}` -- itself `$(shell python3 -c "from aie.utils.config import
root_path; print(root_path())")`, which returns a backslash-separated Windows path --
directly into an unquoted `-I` flag. Git Bash's `sh` (the shell GNU Make resolves to
when invoked from this environment) strips backslashes it doesn't recognize as
escapes, silently mangling the include path and producing a "aie_api/aie.hpp file not
found" error with no indication the path itself was the problem. Fixed at invocation
time by overriding the variable with its forward-slash equivalent:
`make MLIR_AIE_DIR=/c/Users/<user>/mlir-aie/ironenv/Lib/site-packages/mlir_aie ...`.
Not a mlir-aie bug to fix upstream -- it's a Windows-path-through-POSIX-shell
interaction specific to running these Makefiles via Git Bash rather than WSL, the same
class of issue as the getwslpath override already documented.

--- ml/mobilenet (MobileNetV3, IRON) ---

README states plainly: "End-to-end MobileNet V3 inference on the Strix NPU2." Checked
every lit in the directory before running anything:
  - run_e2e.lit, run_strix_makefile.lit: both `REQUIRES: ryzen_ai_npu2` -- Strix only,
    do not apply to this Phoenix (npu1) machine. Not run; this is a chip-generation
    gap, not a local setup gap, same as block_datatypes/ and the other npu2-only
    designs already logged as out of scope in mlir_aie_ml_examples_npu.log.
  - run_numpy_per_bn.lit: `REQUIRES: peano` only, no hardware tag -- pure-numpy
    cross-validation of mobilenet_numpy.py's kernels against brevitas-generated
    per-block fixtures already checked into bottleneck_{A,B,C}/data/. This is the one
    piece of ml/mobilenet actually executable on this machine, and it touches no NPU
    hardware at all -- recorded here for completeness, not as an NPU result:

  python -X utf8 -m mobilenet.test_numpy_per_bn  ->
    bn1 (per-bn)            75264/75264   100.0%  BIT-EXACT
    bn2 (per-bn)            75264/75264   100.0%  BIT-EXACT
    bn3 (per-bn)            31360/31360   100.0%  BIT-EXACT
    bn6 (per-bn)            15680/15680   100.0%  BIT-EXACT
    bn7 (per-bn)            15680/15680   100.0%  BIT-EXACT
    bn8 (per-bn)            15680/15680   100.0%  BIT-EXACT
    bn10->bn11->bn12 chain   3920/3920    100.0%  BIT-EXACT
    bn13->bn14 cascade       3920/3920    100.0%  BIT-EXACT
    ALL VERIFIED BLOCKS BIT-EXACT

--- Conclusion ---

resnet/layers_conv2_x and both magika groups (run_py + the NPU-dispatch half of
trace_py) PASS on this machine's actual XDNA1 (Phoenix) NPU. magika's upstream XFAIL
did not reproduce as a numeric failure here -- worth noting as a discrepancy between
mlir-aie's own CI expectation and this measurement, not silently smoothed over.
mobilenet's hardware paths are Strix-only by the project's own README and REQUIRES
tags; only its numpy-only reference check could run here, and it is not an NPU
measurement.

With this log, every mlir-aie programming_examples/ml design tagged for this machine's
chip (ryzen_ai_npu1) has now been tried on the actual hardware: eltwise, eltwise_unary,
scale_shift, softmax, swiglu (prior log), bottleneck, conv2d, conv2d_14x14 (prior log),
resnet/layers_conv2_x and magika group0/group2 (this log). mobilenet remains the one
ml/ design whose hardware-relevant paths require Strix (npu2), a chip this machine does
not have.

Machine: Desktop 2 / Phoenix.
